have really stuck out for you?

00:21:57.158 --> 00:21:59.838
- Yeah, for me, I mean,
yeah, as you said earlier,

00:21:59.838 --> 00:22:00.858
I've had more of those moments

00:22:00.858 --> 00:22:02.918
'cause it's been a while.

00:22:02.918 --> 00:22:04.508
But one thing that stuck out

00:22:04.508 --> 00:22:07.068
to me was my acceptance speech,

00:22:07.068 --> 00:22:09.058
it was a really nerve wracking moment.

00:22:09.058 --> 00:22:11.478
I wrote my acceptance
speech on the plane ride

00:22:11.478 --> 00:22:13.763
on the way to California.

00:22:13.763 --> 00:22:17.288
So I really poured my heart out for

00:22:17.288 --> 00:22:19.228
that really short acceptance speech.

00:22:19.228 --> 00:22:21.308
And I was really happy that I got

00:22:21.308 --> 00:22:22.441
to say that in front of the world

00:22:22.441 --> 00:22:24.728
and it was a live
streamed and all of that.

00:22:24.728 --> 00:22:27.828
And it was a really amazing moment for me.

00:22:27.828 --> 00:22:29.048
It's like winning the Oscars,

00:22:29.048 --> 00:22:30.858
but in Science and for
high school students.

00:22:30.858 --> 00:22:33.528
So that was really for me.

00:22:33.528 --> 00:22:37.399
And another thing that stuck
out to me was when I talked to

00:22:37.399 --> 00:22:40.312
a bunch of elementary school
kids in the Philippines.

00:22:40.312 --> 00:22:44.178
So I had gotten invited to that event.

00:22:44.178 --> 00:22:45.458
It was a very impromptive event.

00:22:45.458 --> 00:22:48.338
And one of the organizers of that heard

00:22:48.338 --> 00:22:49.305
that I had won the challenge

00:22:49.305 --> 00:22:51.621
and they invited me to
talk to all these kids.

00:22:51.621 --> 00:22:53.828
They were really tiny and
they were fourth grade

00:22:53.828 --> 00:22:56.578
or sixth grade and they
were also energetic.

00:22:56.578 --> 00:22:58.886
And I talked to them
about Science and about

00:22:58.886 --> 00:23:01.538
the Hubble space telescope
and all of these cool things.

00:23:01.538 --> 00:23:03.763
And they were just so energetic
they had so many questions

00:23:03.763 --> 00:23:06.368
and I feel like it was at that moment

00:23:06.368 --> 00:23:08.508
when you really realize that talking

00:23:08.508 --> 00:23:10.828
to people about Science
is really exciting to me,

00:23:10.828 --> 00:23:13.548
and I really find so much meaning in it.

00:23:13.548 --> 00:23:16.938
So I really, I decided that I also wanted

00:23:16.938 --> 00:23:18.728
to do communication aside

00:23:18.728 --> 00:23:22.548
from just pure Physics, so yeah.

00:23:22.548 --> 00:23:23.842
- Yeah and you mentioned,

00:23:23.842 --> 00:23:24.938
it's kind of like the Oscars

00:23:24.938 --> 00:23:26.778
and Yuri Milner who's
really the driving force

00:23:26.778 --> 00:23:27.958
behind the Breakthrough Junior Challenge.

00:23:27.958 --> 00:23:28.809
That was his whole intent,

00:23:28.809 --> 00:23:32.538
which is we celebrate
people in music and movies,

00:23:32.538 --> 00:23:34.678
but really there are people like changing

00:23:34.678 --> 00:23:36.818
the frontiers of Science and mathematics

00:23:36.818 --> 00:23:39.208
that are doing things that are...

00:23:39.208 --> 00:23:41.058
I like movie stars too,

00:23:41.058 --> 00:23:42.088
but that are doing things

00:23:42.088 --> 00:23:43.278
that are gonna change humanity

00:23:43.278 --> 00:23:44.458
and benefit humanity.

00:23:44.458 --> 00:23:45.848
They needed to be awarded the same way.

00:23:45.848 --> 00:23:48.909
And also young people who
can communicate Science,

00:23:48.909 --> 00:23:50.877
when you communicate Science.

00:23:50.877 --> 00:23:53.500
That's how I always think
about my job at Khan Academy.

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.212
I do still secretly hope

00:23:55.212 --> 00:23:56.968
that I might be able to like create

00:23:56.968 --> 00:23:58.078
some new math theorem

00:23:58.078 --> 00:24:00.669
or find some new particle or something.

00:24:00.669 --> 00:24:02.342
But I also like to think that,

00:24:02.342 --> 00:24:04.469
by communicating the Science

00:24:04.469 --> 00:24:05.938
that there's going to be folks

00:24:05.938 --> 00:24:07.338
like y'all millions of them.

00:24:07.338 --> 00:24:09.168
And one of y'all are going to figure out

00:24:09.168 --> 00:24:11.748
how to travel at warp speed

00:24:11.748 --> 00:24:16.288
or solve climate change or cure cancer.

00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:17.121
And so I think that's the real power

00:24:17.121 --> 00:24:18.482
of Science Communication,

00:24:18.482 --> 00:24:22.491
which both of y'all are
already on a fast track,

00:24:24.078 --> 00:24:25.713
maybe one final question for both of you,

00:24:25.713 --> 00:24:30.713
what advice would you have for
a young person like yourself

00:24:30.928 --> 00:24:32.448
a year or two or three years ago?

00:24:32.448 --> 00:24:34.828
Who's like well yeah,
I think I could do it,

00:24:34.828 --> 00:24:36.738
but gee, it's so hard,
a lot of your doubts

00:24:36.738 --> 00:24:38.718
that y'all had maybe start with you,

00:24:38.718 --> 00:24:39.938
Hillary, what advice would you have

00:24:39.938 --> 00:24:41.798
for someone who is thinking about it,

00:24:41.798 --> 00:24:43.718
or maybe is a little bit afraid

00:24:43.718 --> 00:24:45.441
to throw their hat in the ring?

00:24:46.338 --> 00:24:50.036
- Yeah so my first advice
would be to just not be afraid.

00:24:50.036 --> 00:24:53.208
I always say this, and he said
this earlier, when you fail,

00:24:53.208 --> 00:24:54.268
you don't really fail.

00:24:54.268 --> 00:24:55.107
You only learn.

00:24:55.107 --> 00:24:57.488
I have said that a number of times,

00:24:57.488 --> 00:25:00.298
and I'm always surprised
at how much it resonates

00:25:00.298 --> 00:25:02.238
with people, because I mean, we all feel

00:25:02.238 --> 00:25:03.928
that way sometimes, right?

00:25:03.928 --> 00:25:05.831
We feel like we've kind of failed,

00:25:05.831 --> 00:25:08.936
but if you look at it from
a different perspective,

00:25:08.936 --> 00:25:11.791
take it as a learning experience,
a learning opportunity,

00:25:11.791 --> 00:25:13.958
then it won't feel too bad at all.

00:25:13.958 --> 00:25:15.559
And then it would make you even braver

00:25:15.559 --> 00:25:19.287
to pursue better opportunities
to enrich yourself,

00:25:19.287 --> 00:25:21.788
to meet new people and
all of these things.

00:25:21.788 --> 00:25:25.788
So definitely don't take
anything as a failure,

00:25:25.788 --> 00:25:28.691
take it as a learning
experience. That's my advice.

00:25:29.948 --> 00:25:31.398
- And what about you, Maryam?

00:25:34.208 --> 00:25:38.248
- I mean, I found out about
the challenge five years ago

00:25:38.248 --> 00:25:41.018
and I didn't think I
could get anywhere with it

00:25:41.941 --> 00:25:44.898
because I was always like
intimidated by the other videos

00:25:44.898 --> 00:25:47.838
and the other like older
teams and everything.

00:25:47.838 --> 00:25:50.038
But if you're, if someone watching,

00:25:50.038 --> 00:25:51.968
you're thinking about participating

00:25:51.968 --> 00:25:53.098
in a Breakthrough Junior Challenge,

00:25:53.098 --> 00:25:55.379
I just wanna let you know
you it doesn't take a lot.

00:25:55.379 --> 00:25:58.584
I was working on like a
half a broken computer

00:25:58.584 --> 00:26:00.558
